+# Contract: Command-Line Interface
+
+**Branch**: `001-logredact-cli` | **Spec**: [spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This is the tool's only external interface. It is the contract tests assert against; recognition
+rules are specified separately in [redaction-rules.md](./redaction-rules.md).
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. No installation, configuration file, or network access (FR-001,
+SC-009). Program name in all diagnostics is `logredact`, not `__main__.py`.
+
+### Operands and options
+
+| Token | Meaning |
+|-------|---------|
+| `FILE` | A path to read. Zero or more. Read in the exact order given; a repeat is read again (FR-002) |
+| `--count` | Print a tally to stderr after all output. Accepted before, among, or after operands (FR-004) |
+| anything else beginning with `-` | Unrecognised option → usage diagnostic on stderr, exit 2. Never treated as a file name (FR-005) |
+
+With zero `FILE` operands, standard input is read and is the only input consumed (FR-003). With one
+or more, standard input is **not** read at all (US2 scenario 2).
+
+## Streams
+
+| Stream | Contents |
+|--------|----------|
+| stdin | Log text, read only when no `FILE` operand is given |
+| stdout | Every input line, in input order, with redactions applied and nothing else altered (FR-006). Never carries the summary (FR-020) |
+| stderr | Nothing at all on a successful run without `--count` (FR-019); exactly one summary line with `--count` (FR-017); a diagnostic on failure (FR-021) |
+
+**Ordering guarantee**: the summary line is written only after all redacted output has been written
+and stdout flushed (FR-018, US3 scenario 6).
+
+**Fidelity guarantee**: for input containing nothing to redact, stdout is byte-identical to the
+concatenated input, including leading/trailing whitespace, `\r` before a line ending, and the absence
+of a trailing newline on a final line that had none (FR-007, SC-004). Bytes that are not valid UTF-8
+are reproduced unchanged (Edge Cases).
+
+**Determinism**: output depends only on input text; two runs on the same input are identical
+(FR-016).
+
+## Summary format
+
+Exactly one line, written to stderr, terminated by `\n`:
+
+```text
+emails=<N> ips=<N> cards=<N>
+```
+
+Each `<N>` is a decimal count of *replacements performed* for that category over all input read in
+the run — not distinct values, and summed across every file (FR-017; US3 scenarios 3 and 4). Zero is
+printed as `0`, not omitted.
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Condition |
+|--------|-----------|
+| 0 | All requested input was read and written successfully (FR-023) |
+| 2 | A named file could not be opened or read (FR-021); an unrecognised option was given (FR-005); standard output could not be written, e.g. a closed downstream pipe (research R7) |
+
+No other status is produced. Every non-zero exit writes an explanation to stderr naming the offending
+file where one is involved (SC-007), and produces **no** `--count` summary (FR-022).
+
+## Diagnostics
+
+| Situation | stderr | Exit |
+|-----------|--------|------|
+| File does not exist | `logredact: cannot read 'missing.log': No such file or directory` | 2 |
+| Permission denied | `logredact: cannot read 'secret.log': Permission denied` | 2 |
+| Operand is a directory | `logredact: cannot read 'logs/': Is a directory` | 2 |
+| Unrecognised option | argparse usage block naming the bad option | 2 |
+| stdout closed early | `logredact: cannot write to standard output: Broken pipe`, with no traceback and no interpreter shutdown noise | 2 |
+
+Each file diagnostic names both the file as the user typed it and the reason (FR-021, US4 scenario 5);
+the reason text comes from the OS error (`OSError.strerror`), so tests match the file name and a
+non-empty reason rather than exact OS wording.
+
+## Worked examples
+
+These are the byte-for-byte checks behind SC-001. Rows 1–3 are the three invocations given in the
+feature description.
+
+| # | Input (stdin unless stated) | Command | stdout | stderr | Exit |
+|---|------|---------|--------|--------|------|
+| 1 | `user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7\n` | `python -m logredact` | `user [EMAIL] from [IP]\n` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 2 | `paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123\n` | `python -m logredact` | `paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123\n` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 3 | `a@b.io 1.2.3.4\n` | `python -m logredact --count` | `[EMAIL] [IP]\n` | `emails=1 ips=1 cards=0\n` | 0 |
+| 4 | `contact jane.doe+test@mail.example.com\n` | `python -m logredact` | `contact [EMAIL]\n` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 5 | `host 999.1.1.1 unreachable\n` | `python -m logredact` | *(input unchanged)* | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 6 | *(empty)* | `python -m logredact` | *(empty)* | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 7 | *(empty)* | `python -m logredact --count` | *(empty)* | `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0\n` | 0 |
+| 8 | files `a.log`, `b.log` | `python -m logredact a.log b.log` | redacted `a.log` then redacted `b.log` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 9 | file `a.log` readable, `nope.log` missing | `python -m logredact a.log nope.log` | redacted `a.log` | diagnostic naming `nope.log` | 2 |
+| 10 | file `a.log` | `python -m logredact a.log --count` | redacted `a.log` | one summary line | 0 |
+
+## Requirement coverage map
+
+Every functional requirement maps to the test file that must name it (Principle III, SC-002).
+
+| Requirement | Covered by |
+|-------------|-----------|
+| FR-001 runnable as `python -m logredact` | `tests/test_stdin.py` |
+| FR-002 files in order, as one stream | `tests/test_files.py` |
+| FR-003 stdin only when no operand | `tests/test_stdin.py`, `tests/test_files.py` |
+| FR-004 `--count` any position, 0..N operands | `tests/test_count.py` |
+| FR-005 unrecognised option → exit 2 | `tests/test_errors.py` |
+| FR-006 every line written with redactions | `tests/test_redact_line.py`, `tests/test_stdin.py` |
+| FR-007 untouched line reproduced exactly | `tests/test_fidelity.py` |
+| FR-008, FR-009 email replacement and grammar | `tests/test_redact_line.py` |
+| FR-010, FR-011 IPv4 replacement and grammar | `tests/test_redact_line.py` |
+| FR-012, FR-013 card replacement, length, Luhn | `tests/test_redact_line.py`, `tests/test_luhn.py` |
+| FR-014 every occurrence on a line | `tests/test_redact_line.py` |
+| FR-015 left-to-right precedence, no re-use | `tests/test_redact_line.py` |
+| FR-016 deterministic output | `tests/test_redact_line.py` |
+| FR-017 summary format and totals | `tests/test_count.py` |
+| FR-018 summary after all output | `tests/test_count.py` |
+| FR-019 silent stderr without `--count` | `tests/test_count.py` |
+| FR-020 summary not on stdout | `tests/test_count.py` |
+| FR-021 unreadable file: name, reason, exit 2 | `tests/test_errors.py` |
+| FR-022 no summary after failure | `tests/test_errors.py` |
+| FR-023 exit 0 on success | `tests/test_files.py`, `tests/test_stdin.py` |
+| SC-008 performance and flat memory | `tests/test_performance.py` |
