+# Contract: Recognition Grammar and Precedence
+
+**Branch**: `001-logredact-cli` | **Spec**: [spec.md](../spec.md) | **Research**: [research.md](../research.md)
+
+The normative recognition contract. Every pattern below was executed against every example in the
+spec before this document was written; all 25 cases produce the stated result.
+
+## Scanning contract (FR-014, FR-015, FR-016)
+
+Each line is scanned once, left to right. At each position the first category that matches wins, in
+the order **email → IPv4 → card**. Text consumed by a replacement is never reconsidered. A card
+candidate that fails validation is **not** a replacement: scanning resumes one character after that
+candidate's start, so text it covered remains eligible (research R2 — this is what makes
+`123 1.2.3.4` produce `123 [IP]`).
+
+Expressed as one compiled pattern whose alternation order carries the precedence:
+
+```python
+COMBINED = re.compile(f"(?P<email>{EMAIL})|(?P<ip>{IP})|(?P<card>{CARD})")
+```
+
+Rearranging these three alternatives changes behaviour and breaks FR-015.
+
+## Email (FR-008, FR-009)
+
+```python
+COMPONENT  = r"(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4]\d|[01]\d\d|\d\d|\d)"
+IPV4_BODY  = COMPONENT + r"(?:\." + COMPONENT + r"){3}"
+EMAIL      = r"[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@(?:" + IPV4_BODY + r"|(?:[A-Za-z0-9-]+\.)*[A-Za-z]{2,})"
+```
+
+Replacement: `[EMAIL]`.
+
+- Local part: one or more of letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `%`, `+`, `-`. Greedy and unguarded, so
+  adjacent local-part characters are absorbed into the redaction (errs toward removing more).
+- Domain: ordinary labels of letters, digits, `-` separated by `.`, ending in a label of **at least
+  two letters** — *or* a bare IPv4 address. The IPv4 branch implements the spec's `user@1.2.3.4` →
+  `[EMAIL]` requirement and is the one documented deviation from FR-009 as literally worded
+  (research R4).
+
+| Input | Result |
+|-------|--------|
+| `a@b.io` | `[EMAIL]` |
+| `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` | `[EMAIL]` |
+| `user@1.2.3.4` | `[EMAIL]` — counted as 1 email, 0 ips |
+| `jane@example.com.` | `[EMAIL].` — the trailing sentence period is not part of the domain |
+
+## IPv4 (FR-010, FR-011)
+
+```python
+IP = r"(?<![\w.])" + IPV4_BODY + r"(?![\w.])"
+```
+
+Replacement: `[IP]`.
+
+- Exactly four dot-separated components, each 1–3 digits with value 0–255. Leading zeros are accepted
+  (`010.0.0.1` is an address).
+- **Boundary**: rejected if immediately preceded or followed by a word character or a dot. This
+  single rule covers both the spec's "digit or dot" wording and its explicit exclusion of
+  `v1.2.3.4` (research R3), and the lookbehind is what prevents a fragment such as `99.1.1.1` being
+  matched inside `999.1.1.1`.
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|-------|--------|-----|
+| `1.2.3.4`, `10.0.0.7`, `255.255.255.255`, `010.0.0.1` | `[IP]` | valid |
+| `999.1.1.1` | unchanged | no fragment may match |
+| `256.0.0.1` | unchanged | component out of range |
+| `1.2.3` | unchanged | three components |
+| `1.2.3.4.5` | unchanged | trailing dot fails the boundary |
+| `1.2.3.45678` | unchanged | trailing digit fails the boundary |
+| `v1.2.3.4` | unchanged | leading word character fails the boundary |
+
+**Known consequence**: an address followed by a sentence-ending period (`... at 1.2.3.4.`) is left
+unredacted, because the boundary rule cannot distinguish that dot from the fifth component of
+`1.2.3.4.5`, which the spec requires to stay unchanged. Flagged to the spec owner in research R3.
+
+## Payment card (FR-012, FR-013)
+
+```python
+CARD = r"(?<!\d)\d+(?:[ -]\d+)*(?!\d)"
+```
+
+Replacement: `[CARD]`, but only after code validation of the matched run:
+
+1. Remove every space and hyphen.
+2. Require **13 ≤ digit count ≤ 19**.
+3. Require the digits to pass Luhn: right to left, double every second digit, subtract 9 from any
+   doubled value over 9, sum, require total ≡ 0 (mod 10).
+
+Failing either step leaves the run unchanged and increments nothing.
+
+- The pattern matches a **maximal** run, so the whole run is judged — a 20-digit run is never
+  redacted even though a 19-digit window inside it might pass Luhn.
+- A separator is a *single* space or hyphen; `4111  1111` (doubled) ends the number, leaving two
+  short runs that each fail the length rule.
+- Separators may be mixed and group sizes are unconstrained: `4111-1111 1111-1111` is one card.
+- `.` is not a separator, so card matching never spans an IPv4 address.
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|-------|--------|-----|
+| `4111111111111111` | `[CARD]` | 16 digits, Luhn ok |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111` | `[CARD]` | single-space groups |
+| `4111-1111-1111-1111` | `[CARD]` | single-hyphen groups |
+| `4111-1111 1111-1111` | `[CARD]` | mixed separators |
+| `4111  1111 1111 1111` | unchanged | doubled separator splits the run |
+| `1234567890123` | unchanged | 13 digits, Luhn fails |
+| `41111111111111111111` | unchanged | 20 digits |
+| `123 1.2.3.4` | `123 [IP]` | rejected run does not consume the address |
+
+## Cross-category and whole-line contract
+
+| Input | Result |
+|-------|--------|
+| `a@b.io,1.2.3.4` | `[EMAIL],[IP]` — punctuation preserved |
+| `a@b.io a@b.io a@b.io` | three `[EMAIL]` — every occurrence, 3 counted |
+| a line that is one sensitive value | exactly the placeholder |
+| blank / whitespace-only line | reproduced exactly, including tabs and trailing spaces |
+| line with non-UTF-8 bytes | those bytes reproduced unchanged, never redacted |
+| value split across a line ending | not redacted — recognition is line-local |
